    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I've implemented `build_context` in `app/context.py`, and all 7 tests pass (the 4 existing ones plus 3 new ones).\n\n**How it works**\n- It calls `retriever.invoke(question, config={\"tags\": [TRACE_TAG]})`. `invoke` is the standard entry point for every LangChain retriever, so it works the same with `KeywordRetriever` and a vector-store retriever. I checked the installed langchain-core 1.6.5 source: tags passed in the config go onto the retriever's trace run.\n- It goes through the documents in the order the retriever returned them and skips any whose `metadata[\"source\"]` was already used. It stops once it has `MAX_SOURCES` entries.\n- Each entry is `\"[n] <source>\\n<text with surrounding whitespace removed>\"`, and entries are joined with one blank line. If nothing is found, the join returns `\"\"`.\n\n**Tests added** in `tests/test_context.py`:\n- **Duplicates and limit:** a repeated source is skipped, entries are capped at 3, and numbering and whitespace removal are correct.\n- **Nothing found:** returns an empty string.\n- **Trace tag:** the retriever run appears in traces tagged with `kb-context`.\n\nMy first version of the trace-tag test failed because it attached a callback handler to the retriever with `KeywordRetriever(callbacks=...)`. In this version, `invoke` ignores callbacks set on the retriever itself and only uses ones from the call's config or the surrounding context. I rewrote the test to use `collect_runs()`, which picks up runs the same way a real tracer does.\n\nNo dependencies were added."
